A rake, shovel, spade and a hoe.

20
00:00:53,290 --> 00:00:53,430
Right.

21
00:00:53,790 --> 00:00:56,030
Well it seems like you have all your bases covered.

22
00:00:56,570 --> 00:00:57,030
What's next?

23
00:00:57,690 --> 00:00:59,570
I'll till the soil and then sow the seeds.

24
00:01:00,090 --> 00:01:03,330
I'll then add some fertilizer and voila, gardening all done.

25
00:01:03,780 --> 00:01:09,940
Well, good luck with your garden, especially considering we're in the dry season and it won't rain for the next three months.

26
00:01:11,480 --> 00:01:17,160
All right, we're back.

27
00:01:17,160 --> 00:01:19,150
So a lot of vocab related to gardening.

28
00:01:19,500 --> 00:01:24,150
We mentioned a lot of tools and we're going to explain a lot of those now on Language Takeaway.

29
00:01:26,560 --> 00:01:30,560
Okay, so gardening has some very special tools, like you said, Marco.

30
00:01:30,560 --> 00:01:40,640
The first one is one that I am not, well, it's actually not a tool, it's a part of gardening, something that we use to help the plants grow and I really don't like it because it smells bad.

31
00:01:40,640 --> 00:01:41,640
Exactly.

32
00:01:41,640 --> 00:01:48,040
It smells kind of bad and it has a very strong smell and that is called fertilizer.

33
00:01:49,220 --> 00:01:49,440
Fertilizer.

34
00:01:49,440 --> 00:01:56,560
So basically, fertilizer is something that you use in your garden to help the plants grow better.

35
00:01:56,560 --> 00:02:02,920
Right, I think it has a lot of vitamins and minerals that the plant needs to grow better, faster, stronger.

36
00:02:02,920 --> 00:02:04,560
Exactly, fertilizer.

37
00:02:04,560 --> 00:02:06,040
Okay, good.

38
00:02:06,040 --> 00:02:11,280
Well, now we're going to mention four tools that we use in gardening, basic tools.

39
00:02:11,280 --> 00:02:13,560
The first one being a rake.

40
00:02:13,560 --> 00:02:14,560
A rake.

41
00:02:14,560 --> 00:02:15,560
Rake.

42
00:02:15,580 --> 00:02:15,810
Rake.

43
00:02:16,560 --> 00:02:23,560
So a rake is actually something that's very common in most houses, especially houses with a yard or a garden.

44
00:02:23,560 --> 00:02:33,960
It's not just a gardening tool, but it is something that has a long handle and we use it to clear the ground of rocks or leaves.

45
00:02:33,960 --> 00:02:39,880
Right, so just imagine it's kind of like a big brush for your garden.

46
00:02:39,880 --> 00:02:40,880
It is.

47
00:02:40,880 --> 00:02:41,880
It collects things, right?

48
00:02:41,880 --> 00:02:51,320
Right, it's like a big brush that's pulling things out of the ground or away from the ground, especially in the fall, like October or November when all the leaves fall off.

49
00:02:51,320 --> 00:02:58,350
Like October or November when all the leaves fall off the trees and you have to take them off the grass, you use a rake to pull them.

50
00:02:58,760 --> 00:03:04,330
So you use a rake to pull them and you also call the action of using a rake, you say, you rake the garden.

51
00:03:04,840 --> 00:03:06,050
To rake, that's right.

52
00:03:06,520 --> 00:03:07,520
To rake the leaves.

53
00:03:07,520 --> 00:03:09,160
I hate raking the leaves.

54
00:03:09,160 --> 00:03:15,680
Alright, and now the other tool that we have is very common, not only in gardening, but for construction work, for many things.

55
00:03:15,680 --> 00:03:16,510
You have a shovel.

56
00:03:17,160 --> 00:03:17,590
Shovel.

57
00:03:18,050 --> 00:03:21,650
So a shovel is basically a big metal spoon.

58
00:03:22,730 --> 00:03:25,450
And you use it to make a hole in the ground.

59
00:03:26,120 --> 00:03:26,340
Exactly.

60
00:03:27,120 --> 00:03:37,840
So, as we say, we use it a lot in gardening to make holes for maybe the plants, but also in construction, if you want to make a hole in the ground, things like this, you use a shovel.

61
00:03:37,840 --> 00:03:42,970
And now a smaller version of the shovel that you use only with your hand, this is called a spade.

62
00:03:43,480 --> 00:03:44,110
A spade.

63
00:03:44,480 --> 00:03:49,920
Now this is very small, like you said, and it's actually special because of its shape.

64
00:03:49,930 --> 00:03:51,810
A spade has a point.

65
00:03:52,290 --> 00:03:54,590
A shovel is round and a spade has a point.

66
00:03:55,270 --> 00:03:58,450
And what's interesting, Marco, is it's actually very common somewhere else.

67
00:03:59,440 --> 00:03:59,670
In cards.

68
00:04:00,440 --> 00:04:01,440
That's right, in cards.

69
00:04:01,440 --> 00:04:02,590
So cards have different shapes.

70
00:04:03,160 --> 00:04:05,080
One is a heart, one is a diamond.

71
00:04:05,080 --> 00:04:06,440
One of them is a spade.

72
00:04:06,440 --> 00:04:12,640
So the one that's pointy, that's called a spade, and it's used for digging kind of precision holes.

73
00:04:12,640 --> 00:04:13,640
Right, exactly.

74
00:04:13,640 --> 00:04:21,320
So you don't want to use a big shovel if you want to make maybe a small hole in the ground, you would use a spade.

75
00:04:21,320 --> 00:04:25,170
And now our last tool has kind of a funny spelling.

76
00:04:25,600 --> 00:04:28,880
It's H-O-E, and it's pronounced hoe.

77
00:04:28,880 --> 00:04:29,880
Hoe.

78
00:04:29,880 --> 00:04:38,010
So again, this is a tool with a very long handle, and you use it to maybe make the ground softer or to turn up the ground.

79
00:04:38,320 --> 00:04:43,950
So when you're first starting to plant a garden, the ground is very hard, very tough, right?

80
00:04:44,520 --> 00:04:50,160
And this is a really important tool for that period because you can basically turn up the ground.

81
00:04:50,160 --> 00:04:53,750
You're not digging anything, but you're mixing up the dirt a little bit to make it softer.

82
00:04:54,450 --> 00:04:55,110
Okay, very good.

83
00:04:55,370 --> 00:04:57,010
So that's when you use this tool.

84
00:04:57,210 --> 00:04:59,290
It's kind of a dirt or a soil mixer.

85
00:04:59,690 --> 00:05:02,870
You can also pull out those nasty weeds with a hoe.

86
00:05:03,170 --> 00:05:03,810
Okay, good.

87
00:05:04,520 --> 00:05:09,400
So four key tools that we've just taken a look at now.

88
00:05:09,400 --> 00:05:16,560
But now let's move on to two verbs that are basically very important when you're talking about gardening and all this.

89
00:05:18,160 --> 00:05:20,640
As you mentioned, you talked about mixing the soil.

90
00:05:21,440 --> 00:05:25,120
And in the next line, we see that it says, I'll till the soil.

91
00:05:25,620 --> 00:05:25,920
To till.

92
00:05:26,620 --> 00:05:31,840
Okay, so basically this is a way of turning up that soil, but you're doing it kind of in lines, right?

93
00:05:32,400 --> 00:05:34,400
So you're mixing up the soil and making it soft.

94
00:05:34,640 --> 00:05:37,520
You're preparing the soil for planting.

95
00:05:37,520 --> 00:05:39,920
And so you till the soil.

96
00:05:39,920 --> 00:05:41,620
Soil of course, in this case, is that dirt.

97
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:42,220
It's earth.

98
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:43,160
Right, it's earth.

99
00:05:44,040 --> 00:05:46,580
So to till is to prepare it for planting.

100
00:05:47,280 --> 00:05:48,120
Till the soil, that's right.

101
00:05:48,280 --> 00:05:49,340
To till the soil, okay.

102
00:05:49,340 --> 00:05:51,820
And what do we do after the soil has been tilled?

103
00:05:52,400 --> 00:05:54,940
Well then we sow the seeds.

104
00:05:55,640 --> 00:05:58,180
Okay, so seeds are baby plants basically.

105
00:05:59,140 --> 00:06:00,840
And we sow the seeds.

106
00:06:01,440 --> 00:06:04,600
When we sow the seeds, we're putting the seeds into the ground.

107
00:06:05,660 --> 00:06:08,260
So that's basically planting the seeds.

108
00:06:08,380 --> 00:06:12,080
You can say we plant the seeds or we sow the seeds.

109
00:06:12,900 --> 00:06:13,960
And the opposite of sowing?

110
00:06:14,480 --> 00:06:16,820
The opposite of sowing is reaping.

111
00:06:16,920 --> 00:06:19,880
R-E-A-P, to reap.

112
00:06:19,880 --> 00:06:21,300
So we actually have a phrase in English.

113
00:06:21,720 --> 00:06:23,280
This is very, very famous, very common.

114
00:06:23,840 --> 00:06:26,130
We say, you reap what you sow.

115
00:06:26,760 --> 00:06:28,090
You reap what you sow, that's right.

116
00:06:28,280 --> 00:06:32,640
So you basically collect what you planted and that's kind of what you do in life.

117
00:06:32,640 --> 00:06:36,250
So we say if you haven't planted anything good, you're not going to reap anything good.

118
00:06:36,720 --> 00:06:36,990
Exactly.

119
00:06:37,720 --> 00:06:38,770
It's a very popular phrase.

120
